# DriftSense Pilot — Architecture

DriftSense Pilot localises a reference patch on a periodic semiconductor array
(FinFET, DRAM-like contacts, grids) where classical template matching sees many
identical-looking copies. Three views follow: the deployed system on AWS with the
COOL worker, the OpenCV 5 engine stages S0–S9 as they are implemented in
`driftsense/v2/`, and the agent loop that decides between committing a stage
move, re-acquiring, and escalating to a human.

## 1. System on AWS (ap-south-1) with COOL

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  user["Browser (judge / engineer)"] --> cf["CloudFront"]
  cf --> ui[("S3: static UI bucket (OAC, private)")]
  user --> api["API Gateway HTTP API (throttled, CORS = UI origin)"]
  api --> lam["Lambda arm64, Python 3.12<br/>FastAPI app via Mangum<br/>presign, create job, status, approval"]
  lam --> ddb[("DynamoDB: jobs + trace index (TTL)")]
  lam --> q[["SQS jobs queue"]]
  q -. "redrive after max receives" .-> dlq[["SQS dead-letter queue"]]
  lam --> data[("S3 data bucket<br/>uploads/ jobs/ traces/ expire, demos/ kept<br/>SSE, TLS-only, presigned PUT size cap")]
  subgraph asg["EC2 Auto Scaling group, min 1 / max 2, SG with no inbound, SSM only, IMDSv2"]
    worker["c8g.large Graviton worker<br/>COOL 3.1 AMI<br/>cv2 imported from /opt/cool (guarded)<br/>v2 engine + agent runner + VirtualSEM"]
  end
  q -- "long poll" --> worker
  worker --> data
  worker --> ddb
  worker --> cw["CloudWatch<br/>EMF metrics: JobLatencyMs, StageMs, Jobs*<br/>dashboard + alarms -> SNS"]
  canary["EventBridge schedule -> canary Lambda<br/>GET /healthz, /v1/version"] --> api
  canary --> cw
  budgets["AWS Budgets: monthly cost alerts"] -.-> sns["SNS e-mail"]
  cw -.-> sns
  gh["GitHub Actions (main)"] -- "OIDC, sts:AssumeRole cdk-* only" --> cdk["CDK (Python) deploy"]
  cdk -.-> api
  subgraph bench["Ephemeral bench stack (-c bench=true), self-terminating"]
    b1["x86-pip: c7i.4xlarge, PyPI OpenCV"]
    b2["graviton-pip: c8g.4xlarge, PyPI OpenCV"]
    b3["graviton-cool: c8g.4xlarge, COOL 3.1 AMI"]
  end
  bench -- "run_all.sh uploads to the bench/ prefix" --> data
```

- **No ALB and no NAT gateway**: both are always-on costs. The worker sits in a
  public subnet with a security group that allows no inbound traffic and is
  administered through SSM Session Manager only; there are no SSH keys.
- **Production path is COOL on Arm**: the worker imports OpenCV from the COOL
  virtual environment under `/opt/cool`, never from a PyPI wheel, and refuses to
  start otherwise. The evidence page records each arm's `cv2.__file__`.
- **The API never runs the engine.** Lambda only validates, presigns, enqueues
  and reads status; the OpenCV work happens on the worker, which pulls jobs from
  SQS. A job that keeps failing lands in the dead-letter queue instead of looping.
- **Security**: least-privilege IAM, IMDSv2 required, S3 Block Public Access,
  TLS-only bucket policies, encryption at rest, size-capped presigned uploads,
  API throttling, a cost budget with alerts, and a deploy role that GitHub can
  assume only from `main` through OIDC (no long-lived keys).
- **Bench stack**: three identical-input benchmark instances that upload their
  results to S3 and terminate themselves; it exists only for a benchmark
  campaign (see [COOL_EVIDENCE.md](COOL_EVIDENCE.md)).

## 2. Engine stages S0–S9 (OpenCV 5)

`driftsense.v2.localize()` runs the stages below. ★ marks KleidiCV-listed
operations (the ones COOL can accelerate on Graviton, for supported dtypes and
kernel sizes); unmarked OpenCV calls run the stock implementation.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
  s0["S0 Ingest / QC<br/>imdecode, cvtColor ★, meanStdDev, minMaxLoc ★"]
  s1["S1 Lattice (Fourier)<br/>getOptimalDFTSize, createHanningWindow, dft, magnitude, log, minMaxLoc ★"]
  s2["S2 Rectify (up to four hypotheses)<br/>pyrDown ★, getRotationMatrix2D, warpPerspective ★"]
  rot["De-rotate to the dominant lattice axis<br/>getRotationMatrix2D, warpPerspective ★"]
  s3["S3 Roughness residual<br/>getGaussianKernel, sepFilter2D ★, GaussianBlur ★, Sobel ★,<br/>magnitude, getStructuringElement, erode ★, dilate ★"]
  s4["S4 Lattice-consistent candidates + score<br/>matchTemplate TM_CCOEFF_NORMED, dilate ★ (NMS), minMaxLoc ★"]
  s5["S5 DRAM contour<br/>scaffold in this build: not on the path"]
  s6["S6 Cascade<br/>re-rank top-K by the frozen v1 fingerprint when margin < tau (NumPy)"]
  s7["S7 Confidence<br/>robust z-margin + isotonic calibration -> commit / escalate (NumPy)"]
  s8["S8 Subpixel<br/>createHanningWindow, phaseCorrelate, findTransformECC"]
  s9["S9 Explain<br/>applyColorMap, cvtColor ★, resize ★, addWeighted, drawMarker,<br/>rectangle, putText, copyMakeBorder, hconcat, imencode"]
  out(["LocalizeResult: offset, candidates, margin, decision, timings, artifacts"])
  s0 --> s1 --> s2 --> rot --> s3 --> s4 --> s6 --> s8 --> s7 --> s9 --> out
  s5 -. "planned DRAM branch" .-> s4
```

| Stage | Module | What it does | OpenCV calls (★ = KleidiCV-listed) |
|---|---|---|---|
| S0 Ingest / QC | `ingest.py` | Decode an 8- or 16-bit frame, grayscale, mean/std, min/max, saturation and an SNR proxy | `imdecode`, `cvtColor` ★, `meanStdDev`, `minMaxLoc` ★ |
| S1 Lattice | `lattice.py` | Windowed DFT, two dominant reciprocal-lattice peaks, period and orientation | `getOptimalDFTSize`, `createHanningWindow`, `dft`, `magnitude`, `log`, `minMaxLoc` ★ |
| S2 Rectify | `rectify.py` | Up to four de-rotation homographies scored on a coarse copy | `pyrDown` ★, `getRotationMatrix2D`, `warpPerspective` ★ |
| De-rotate | `engine.py` | Bring the dominant lattice axis onto an image axis before S3/S4 | `getRotationMatrix2D`, `warpPerspective` ★ |
| S3 Roughness residual | `roughness.py` | Smooth along the lines, keep each edge's line-edge-roughness residual in a dilated edge band | `getGaussianKernel`, `sepFilter2D` ★, `GaussianBlur` ★, `Sobel` ★, `magnitude`, `getStructuringElement`, `erode` ★, `dilate` ★ |
| S4 Candidates + score | `score.py` | Residual-on-residual correlation, kept only at lattice-consistent offsets | `matchTemplate` (`TM_CCOEFF_NORMED`), `getStructuringElement`, `dilate` ★ (NMS), `minMaxLoc` ★ |
| S5 DRAM contour | `contour.py` | Planned contour fingerprint for contact arrays; a typed scaffold in this build | none yet |
| S6 Cascade | `cascade.py` | When the top two are close, re-rank the short-list by the frozen v1 fingerprint | none (NumPy) |
| S7 Confidence | `confidence.py` | Robust z-margin and an isotonic calibrator fit on a separate calibration split; commit or escalate | none (NumPy) |
| S8 Subpixel | `subpixel.py` | Phase correlation seeds an ECC translation refinement | `createHanningWindow`, `phaseCorrelate`, `findTransformECC` (`MOTION_TRANSLATION`) |
| S9 Explain | `explain.py` | Heatmaps, ranked candidate gallery and overlay as PNGs with alt text | `applyColorMap`, `convertScaleAbs`, `cvtColor` ★, `resize` ★, `addWeighted`, `drawMarker`, `rectangle`, `putText`, `copyMakeBorder`, `hconcat`, `imencode` |

**Why S3 works.** Normalised cross-correlation on raw intensity is dominated by
the periodic part of the image, which is identical at every replica.
Subtracting the along-line smoothed image leaves each edge's stochastic
roughness, which is unique to the site; S4 then correlates residual against
residual only at offsets the S1 lattice allows. S6 keeps the v1 fingerprint as a
cascade on the short-list rather than replacing it.

**What is not claimed.** The S5 contour path is not implemented in this build,
so DRAM-like arrays go through the same residual path as line arrays. The hot
path has no per-pixel Python loops; the numerical results reported elsewhere
state which OpenCV build produced them.

## 3. Agent loop: commit, re-acquire or escalate

The default policy (`policy_rules.decide`) is a deterministic ladder over what
the last localisation and the instrument report. The optional LLM planner
(`policy_llm`) may only propose a schema-validated perception tool; commit and
escalate are never its decision. The runner (`runner.run_episode`) enforces the
safety gates whatever any policy says.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  start(["Episode: reference acquire (high dwell) seeds the template"]) --> acq0["acquire(search frame)"]
  acq0 --> loc["localize(): v2 engine -> margin, top candidates"]
  loc --> obs{"Observation: margin vs tau, SNR, lattice / rectify uncertainty,<br/>period ambiguity at FOV edge, step and dose budget"}
  obs -- "margin >= tau" --> gate{"Runner safety gate<br/>margin >= tau re-checked"}
  gate -- pass --> commit["commit_stage_move(dx, dy)<br/>trace: because = 'margin X >= tau Y'"]
  commit --> verify["verify acquire + localize"] --> done(["outcome: commit"])
  gate -- "margin < tau (policy tried anyway)" --> hum
  obs -- "budget exhausted" --> hum["request_human_approval(top3, evidence)"]
  obs -- "low SNR" --> a1["acquire(dwell x2)"]
  obs -- "lattice / rectify uncertain" --> l4["localize(rect_hypotheses=4)"]
  obs -- "top-2 one period apart at FOV edge" --> a2["acquire(fov x2)"]
  obs -- "contact-like / low margin" --> lc["localize(mode=contour)"]
  obs -- otherwise --> rf["refine(): accurate mode"]
  a1 --> loc
  a2 --> loc
  l4 --> obs
  lc --> obs
  rf --> obs
  hum --> card["Approval card (UI) / MCP client"]
  card -- approve --> commit
  card -- reject --> stop(["outcome: escalate, no stage move"])
  llm["Optional LLM planner"] -. "may propose acquire / localize / refine only" .-> obs
  err["Tool timeout, dose overrun or any exception"] --> abort(["outcome: abort or escalate, never a silent commit"])
  loc -. "every step" .-> trace[("TraceEvent JSONL + CloudWatch")]
```

**Hard rules enforced by the runner, not by the policy:**

- never commit a stage move while the margin is below the threshold tau; a
  policy that tries is coerced into a human-approval escalation, and the trace
  records the refusal;
- a fixed cap on decision steps per episode and an electron-dose budget; a
  projected or instrument-raised overrun becomes an escalation;
- a per-tool wall-clock timeout, and any exception aborts the episode safely;
- every step is a contract `TraceEvent`, and the commit event carries the
  margin and tau that justified it.

The six tools (`acquire`, `localize`, `refine`, `commit_stage_move`,
`request_human_approval`, `get_trace`) are pydantic-validated and exposed
unchanged through a FastMCP server (`python -m driftsense.agent.mcp_server`), so
an MCP client drives the virtual SEM through exactly the same gates. The hidden
ground truth (`VirtualSEM.verify`) is reachable only by the evaluator.
